id: 762
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 762, "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "data": {"id": "16ad2c75-7e33-4e03-b292-93dd95b2ae2e", "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "kind": "tool", "position": 760, "revision": 762, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"name": "Read", "input": "{\"file_path\": \"[workspace]\", \"offset\": 49, \"limit\": 35}", "status": "running", "tool_use_id": "call_73b43837f8e142339eeea2a0"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:16:40.598531+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:16:41.334123+00:00"}}

id: 764
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 764, "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "data": {"id": "16ad2c75-7e33-4e03-b292-93dd95b2ae2e", "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "kind": "tool", "position": 760, "revision": 764, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"name": "Read", "input": "{\"file_path\": \"[workspace]\", \"offset\": 49, \"limit\": 35}", "output": "49\t\n50\t## Finding 1: A Narrow Spectral Peak at ~1010–1060 μHz Is Strongly Supported by the Information Criterion Landscape\n51\t\n52\t**Object and scope:** The full candidate spectral parameter grid (250 ranked combinations, 507 raw evaluations across two scan families), evaluated on the complete set of 22 passes.\n53\t\n54\t**Relationship and direction:** The information criterion (IC) landscape shows a sharply localized minimum near peak frequency = 1010 μHz with width = 275 μHz and max velocity = 30 cm/s, min velocity = 0.05 cm/s. This configuration achieves the global minimum IC of 43.9. The next-best candidate (rank 2) has IC = 45.9 (delta_IC = 2.0), differing in both peak frequency (1060 vs. 1010 μHz) and max velocity (20 vs. 30 cm/s). Only 4 of 250 candidates fall within delta_IC ≤ 10 of the best, and only 10 within delta_IC ≤ 20.\n55\t\n56\t**Core quantitative result:** \n57\t- Rank 1: v_max=30 cm/s, v_min=0.05 cm/s, f_peak=1010 μHz, width=275 μHz, IC=43.9\n58\t- Rank 2: v_max=20 cm/s, v_min=0.05 cm/s, f_peak=1060 μHz, width=300 μHz, delta_IC=2.0\n59\t- Rank 3: delta_IC=5.7; Rank 4: delta_IC=9.4\n60\t- Candidates within delta_IC ≤ 10: 4 out of 250 (1.6%)\n61\t\n62\t**Supporting evidence:**\n63\t- `data/ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv` — full ranking with delta IC values\n64\t- `data/candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv` — raw IC landscape showing the sharp minimum\n65\t- `output/result_artifacts.json` — machine-readable spectral analysis results\n66\t- `output/analysis.py` lines 100–140 — candidate spectrum analysis code\n67\t\n68\t**Robustness:** The ranked grid is derived from the complete candidate evaluation; the delta_IC gaps are monotonic and the top-4 cluster is separated from rank 5 (delta_IC=11.9) by a gap of 2.5, which is comparable to the spread among ranks 2–4. The minimum velocity scan independently confirms that the lowest IC values occur at v_min ∈ {0.01, 0.02, 0.03} cm/s with v_max ≥ 30 cm/s, but these are evaluated under a different scan family and use absolute IC (not delta relative to the same reference), so they cannot be directly merged with the ranked grid without knowing the shared baseline.\n69\t\n70\t**Counterevidence and limitations:** The delta_IC gap between rank 1 and rank 2 is only 2.0, which is modest. While only 4 candidates lie within delta_IC ≤ 10, this does not prove physical uniqueness — the DATA_GUIDE explicitly states that \"a lower information criterion supports a relative comparison within the supplied candidate family, not proof that the family is physically unique.\" The frequency_amplitude_scan family (304 rows) dominates the low-IC region, while the minimum_velocity_scan family (203 rows) contributes fewer low-IC candidates.\n71\t\n72\t**Alternative explanations:** The sharp IC minimum could reflect overfitting if the candidate family is flexible enough to absorb noise. However, the consistent preference for frequencies near 1010–1060 μHz across multiple velocity settings argues against pure noise fitting. The narrow width (275 μHz) at rank 1 versus broader widths (300–325 μHz) at other ranks suggests the data prefer a relatively narrow spectral feature.\n73\t\n74\t**Falsification proposal:** Acquire additional passes under identical observational conditions. If the spectral structure is real, the re-ranked candidate grid should continue to place a candidate near 1010 μHz at or near rank 1, with delta_IC to rank 2 remaining small (< 5). If the current ranking is driven by pass-specific noise, adding data should increase the delta_IC gap or shift the preferred frequency.\n75\t\n76\t---\n77\t\n78\t## Finding 2: Residual Variance Is Dominated by Within-Pass Structure With Negligible Between-Pass Coherence\n79\t\n80\t**Object and scope:** All 22 time-resolved passes, three residual models (A, B, C), 1,698 time samples total.\n81\t\n82\t**Relationship and direction:** Variance decomposition reveals that between-pass variance accounts for only 0.7% (Model A), 0.01% (Model B), and 0.05% (Model C) of total variance. Over 99% of the variance resides within individual passes. This means the residuals exhibit rich temporal structure within each pass, but there is no coherent signal shared across passes at the level of pass-mean offsets.\n83\t", "status": "completed", "is_async": false, "tool_use_id": "call_73b43837f8e142339eeea2a0"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:16:40.598531+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:16:41.577804+00:00"}}

