id: 973
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 973, "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "data": {"id": "a7de4fc5-2fa2-47b9-a07e-360bd6df31ac", "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "kind": "tool", "position": 969, "revision": 973, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"name": "Read", "input": "{\"file_path\": \"[workspace]\", \"offset\": 130, \"limit\": 35}", "output": "130\t**Counterevidence and limitations:** High correlation does not imply the models capture the same physics; it may reflect shared numerical properties or similar baseline assumptions. The mean absolute differences between models are small (typically 0.005–0.02 mm/s), meaning all three models produce numerically close outputs despite their structural differences.\n131\t\n132\t**Alternative explanations:** Model B may implement a different regularization scheme, noise model, or physical approximation that produces systematically different temporal patterns while maintaining similar overall magnitude. The negative autocorrelation in Model B (Finding 2) is consistent with this interpretation.\n133\t\n134\t**Falsification proposal:** If Models A and C truly share a deeper structural similarity, then leaving out Model C should degrade fit quality more than leaving out Model B, when evaluated on held-out time segments within each pass. Testing this requires access to the underlying fitting procedure.\n135\t\n136\t---\n137\t\n138\t## Finding 4: The Structured Candidate Family Dramatically Outperforms the Uniform-Velocity Alternative\n139\t\n140\t**Object and scope:** Comparison of the best structured candidate (IC = 43.9) against the entire uniform-velocity alternative curve (24 points, velocities from 0.001 to 500 cm/s).\n141\t\n142\t**Relationship and direction:** The best uniform-velocity model achieves IC = 1522.1 at v = 0.1 cm/s, which is 1,478.2 IC units worse than the best structured candidate (IC = 43.9). The uniform IC curve is U-shaped with a clear minimum at v = 0.1 cm/s and rises monotonically on both sides, reaching IC > 13,500 at v = 500 cm/s. No uniform velocity comes within 1% of the structured candidate's IC.\n143\t\n144\t**Core quantitative result:**\n145\t- Best structured candidate IC: 43.9\n146\t- Best uniform alternative IC: 1522.1 (at v_uniform = 0.1 cm/s)\n147\t- Improvement factor: 1522.1 / 43.9 ≈ 34.7× better IC for structured model\n148\t- Uniform IC at v = 0.1 cm/s is itself the minimum of the uniform curve; all other uniform velocities yield higher IC\n149\t\n150\t**Supporting evidence:**\n151\t- `data/uniform_alternative_comparison.tsv` — uniform velocity IC curve\n152\t- `data/ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv` — best structured candidate\n153\t- `output/generate_artifacts.py` — uniform alternative comparison code (lines 90–110)\n154\t- `output/result_artifacts.json` — uniform alternative section\n155\t\n156\t**Robustness:** The uniform IC curve is smooth and unimodal, with the minimum clearly at v = 0.1 cm/s. The structured candidate's IC of 43.9 is far outside the range spanned by the uniform curve (1522–13553), making the conclusion insensitive to the exact placement of the uniform minimum.\n157\t\n158\t**Counterevidence and limitations:** The IC comparison is valid only within the supplied candidate family. The DATA_GUIDE warns that \"a lower information criterion supports a relative comparison within the supplied candidate family, not proof that the family is physically unique.\" Other parametric families not explored here might achieve even lower IC. Additionally, the uniform alternative uses a single velocity parameter while the structured candidates use four (v_max, v_min, f_peak, width), so the comparison partially reflects model complexity.\n159\t\n160\t**Alternative explanations:** The structured candidate's superior IC could reflect the ability to fit a narrow spectral feature that the uniform model cannot represent, rather than evidence for a real physical spectral line. If the true signal is broad-band, the structured model might overfit.\n161\t\n162\t**Falsification proposal:** Test the structured candidate against additional uniform-like alternatives with different functional forms (e.g., Gaussian, Lorentzian, power-law) using the same IC metric. If these also achieve IC >> 100, the structured candidate's advantage is robust to alternative null models. If a simple alternative achieves IC < 50, the structured interpretation weakens.\n163\t\n164\t---", "status": "completed", "is_async": false, "tool_use_id": "call_e194a1a4c6854852bcfeb9c1"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:17:47.571147+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:17:48.277688+00:00"}}

