id: 979
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 979, "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "data": {"id": "f47e4966-2722-4cf2-a068-2c76fb3acc3f", "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "kind": "text", "position": 979, "revision": 979, "parent_tool_use_id": "call_d16d251545854573999eec06", "agent_call_id": "37dadf21-9c98-4b4a-bc18-6f4096ef20e3", "data": {"text": "All three JSON files are valid. The analysis is complete. Here is the summary:\n\n---\n\n**Analysis Complete -- All Files Saved to `[workspace]`**\n\n**Files saved:**\n- `fft_loo_analysis.py` -- standalone Python script (no imports of existing code)\n- `fft_results.json` -- per-pass FFT top-5 frequencies and cross-pass consistency\n- `loo_results.json` -- leave-one-pass-out robustness statistics\n- `consistency_results.json` -- candidate spectrum cross-pass consistency analysis\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. **FFT Periodicity Analysis (22 passes x 3 models):** No consistent signal near the rank-1 frequency of 1010 muHz was found in any pass. The top peak frequencies across all passes span a wide range (12,500 to 487,500 muHz). The most frequently appearing binned frequencies (37,500, 50,000, 62,500 muHz) appear at regular intervals that correspond to harmonic artifacts of the sampling grid rather than a physical periodic signal. These are likely numerical artifacts from the uniform time spacing of the data, not evidence for a real oscillation at 1010 muHz.\n\n2. **LOO Robustness -- FAILS for pass_01:** Leaving out pass_01 causes >10% relative change in residual std for all 3 models: Model A (-21%), Model B (-42%), Model C (-32%). This confirms pass_01 (std ~0.118 vs others ~0.02-0.03) is a strong outlier driving the overall variance. No other single pass produces >10% changes. LOO stability does NOT hold due to this one pass.\n\n3. **Candidate Spectrum Cross-Pass Consistency:** The rank-1 parameters (v_max=30, v_min=0.05, f_peak=1010 muHz, width=275 muHz) do not appear as an exact match in the `candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv`. The target period of 1010 muHz corresponds to ~59,400 minutes, far exceeding the time span of individual passes (~15-40 minutes), so no single pass has sufficient temporal resolution to resolve this frequency. The IC values in the spectrum comparison are computed over all passes combined, making true per-pass candidate re-ranking impossible with the available data."}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:17:54.382068+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:17:54.382074+00:00"}}

