id: 1021
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1021, "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "data": {"id": "d653b177-984f-4a95-b46b-a0891066a7f6", "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "kind": "tool", "position": 1017, "revision": 1021, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"name": "Read", "input": "{\"file_path\": \"[workspace]\", \"limit\": 30, \"offset\": 100}", "output": "100\t**Supporting evidence:**\n101\t- `data/pass_residual_traces.tsv` — raw residual traces\n102\t- `output/result_artifacts.json` — temporal analysis section with per-pass statistics and variance decomposition\n103\t- `output/analysis.py` lines 50–90 — autocorrelation and variance decomposition code\n104\t\n105\t**Robustness:** Leave-one-pass-out recomputation of mean and standard deviation shows negligible change (remaining mean shifts < 0.0002, std changes < 0.0002), confirming that no single pass drives the aggregate statistics. First-half vs. second-half comparisons show mean differences centered near zero (A: −0.0015, B: +0.0001, C: −0.0005) with small standard deviations, consistent with stationarity within passes.\n106\t\n107\t**Counterevidence and limitations:** The extremely low between-pass fractions could indicate that the residual models have been normalized or differ primarily in their internal parameterization rather than capturing fundamentally different physics. Model B's negative mean autocorrelation (−0.28) contrasts sharply with Model A's positive value (0.53), suggesting that the three models encode different temporal assumptions. This model-dependence limits generalizability of any single-model finding.\n108\t\n109\t**Alternative explanations:** The lack of between-pass coherence could arise because: (a) each pass observes a different realization of stochastic processes, (b) the residual computation removes any common-mode signal, or (c) the system's intrinsic variability is much faster than the pass-to-pass timescale. Without external timing information linking the passes, these alternatives cannot be distinguished.\n110\t\n111\t**Falsification proposal:** If the passes can be ordered temporally (e.g., by observation date) and correlated with an independent time-varying parameter (e.g., orbital phase, stellar activity cycle), then between-pass variance should become significant when conditioned on that parameter. Failure to find such conditioning would support the null interpretation.\n112\t\n113\t---\n114\t\n115\t## Finding 3: Residual Models A and C Show Substantially Higher Cross-Model Agreement Than Model B\n116\t\n117\t**Object and scope:** Pairwise correlations between Models A, B, and C within each of the 22 passes.\n118\t\n119\t**Relationship and direction:** Model A and Model C agree most strongly (mean Pearson r = 0.81), followed by B and C (r = 0.78), while A and B show the weakest agreement (r = 0.57). In 9 of 22 passes, the A–B correlation falls below 0.50, whereas the A–C correlation exceeds 0.80 in 12 of 22 passes. This asymmetry suggests that Model C shares more structural features with Model A than Model B does.\n120\t\n121\t**Core quantitative result:**\n122\t- MA vs MB: mean r = 0.573, std = 0.184, range [0.253, 0.994]\n123\t- MA vs MC: mean r = 0.810, std = 0.115, range [0.575, 0.998]\n124\t- MB vs MC: mean r = 0.783, std = 0.132, range [0.489, 0.998]\n125\t- Passes with A–B r < 0.50: 9 of 22 (pass_05, pass_06, pass_09, pass_11, pass_13, pass_14, pass_18, pass_20, pass_21)\n126\t- Passes with A–C r > 0.80: 12 of 22\n127\t\n128\t**Supporting evidence:**\n129\t- `data/pass_residual_traces.tsv` — raw residual traces", "status": "completed", "is_async": false, "tool_use_id": "call_14266e06736f4f6dbcd6389a"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:18:32.900557+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:18:33.589402+00:00"}}

