is**\"\n- FFT found no consistent signal near 1010 μHz; top peaks at 12,500–487,500 μHz (sampling harmonics)\n- LOO reveals pass_01 is a strong outlier (removing it changes std by −21% to"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:20:16.046236+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:20:19.667903+00:00"}}

id: 1140
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1140, "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "data": {"id": "5b9a0389-0ddb-4dd8-8a68-f4ebb870a6c4", "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "kind": "text", "position": 1131, "revision": 1140, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"text": "All deliverables are in place. Here is a summary of the Round 002 corrections completed:\n\n---\n\n## Round 002 Corrections Summary\n\n### SKILL.md & Reference Modules Read\n- `.claude/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md` — read\n- `references/delegation-contract.md` — read\n- `references/analysis-state.md` — read\n- `references/verification.md` — read\n- `references/delivery.md` — read\n- `references/data-design.md` — read\n- `references/inference.md` — read\n\n### Sub-Agent Delegation (2 independent executors)\n1. **FFT/LOO agent** (`working/subagent_fft_loo/`) — FFT periodicity + leave-one-pass-out robustness\n2. **Verification agent** (`working/subagent_verification/`) — spectral ranking + cross-model correlation re-computation\n\n### Factual Corrections Applied\n| Item | Error | Correction |\n|------|-------|-----------|\n| 4 | IC=43.9 attributed to rank-1 params | Rank-1 params don't exist in candidate_spectrum; IC=43.9 from different params (v_max=20, v_min=0) |\n| 5a | \"10 within delta_IC≤20\" | Corrected to **9** |\n| 5b | \"A-B <0.50 in 10 passes\" | Corrected to **9** |\n| 5b | \"A-C >0.80 in 14 passes\" | Corrected to **12** |\n| 5c | \"B-C max 0.936\" | Corrected to **0.998** |\n| 6 | Coupling \"increases with radial order\" | Corrected to **non-monotonic, peaks at n=4** |\n| 7 | FFT/LOO not saved as artifacts | Saved to `working/subagent_fft_loo/fft_results.json` and `loo_results.json` |\n| 8 | IC ratio \"34.7× better\" | Changed to **IC difference = 1478.2** with baseline comparability argument |\n\n### Key Scientific Reconciliation\n- Finding 1 headline changed from \"Strongly Supported\" to \"**Indicated by IC but Not Confirmed by Temporal Periodicity Analysis**\"\n- FFT found no consistent signal near 1010 μHz; top peaks at 12,500–487,500 μHz (sampling harmonics)\n- LOO reveals pass_01 is a strong outlier (removing it changes std by −21% to −42%)\n- Two data files represent overlapping but distinct candidate pools (only 29/250 overlap)\n\n### Validation\n`python3 validate_output.py output` → **passed: true**, 0 errors, 0 warnings"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:20:16.046236+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:20:20.000578+00:00"}}

id: 1141
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1141, "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "data": {"id": "4b43299c-1909-4626-bc0f-a82a6cd56c67", "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "kind": "agent", "position": 412, "revision": 1141, "parent_tool_use_id": "call_d16d251545854573999eec06", "agent_call_id": "37dadf21-9c98-4b4a-bc18-6f4096ef20e3", "data": {"status": "completed", "agent_type": "general-purpose", "sdk_agent_id": "a5ba94619161a7725", "task": "You are a leaf sub-Agent performing an independent computational analysis branch for the task **Astronomy_02_gravity_spectral_structure** (T0: 2022-01-01). You must NOT delegate to any other agent, session, or model process.\n\n## Your Role and Problem\nPerform two independent computational analyses that support core conclusions about temporal structure in gravity residual data:\n1. **FFT periodicity analysis** — test whether any preferred candidate frequency survives whole-pass perturbations\n2. **Leave-one-pass-out (LOO) robustness** — test whether the ranked candidate ordering survives leaving out each pass\n\nThese are independent computations from the main analysis. Write your own code from scratch using the raw data files. Do not import or execute any existing scripts.\n\n## Project and Inputs\n- Project root: `[workspace]`\n- Data directory: `data/`\n- Read-only files:\n  - `data/pass_residual_traces.tsv` — 22 passes × ~77 time samples, 3 models (A, B, C) in mm/s\n  - `data/ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv` — 250 ranked candidates with delta_information_criterion\n  - `data/candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv` — 507 rows, two scan families\n- Independent analysis unit: one complete time-resolved pass or one complete candidate spectral parameter combination\n\n## Required Context Reads (MUST DO BEFORE ANALYSIS)\nRead these files successfully before starting analysis:\n1. `[workspace]`\n2. `[workspace]`\n3. `[workspace]`\n4. `[workspace]`\n5. `[workspace]`\n\n## Exclusive Output Directory\nSave ALL your work under: `[workspace]`\n- Create the directory if needed\n- Save your Python script as `fft_loo_analysis.py`\n- Save results as `fft_results.json` and `loo_results.json`\n- Do NOT modify any files outside this directory\n\n## Analysis Requirements\n\n### 1. FFT Periodicity Analysis\nFor each of the 22 passes and each of the 3 models:\n- Detrend the residual trace (remove linear trend via polyfit)\n- Compute FFT and find the top-5 peak frequencies (excluding DC component)\n- Convert frequencies to μHz\n- For each pass, also compute the mean of the top peak frequency across all 3 models\n- Identify whether any single frequency appears as a top-peak in more than half of the passes (across any model)\n- Report: per-pass top frequencies, cross-pass consistency, and whether a common periodic signal exists\n\n### 2. Leave-One-Pass-Out Robustness\nFor each pass to leave out (22 iterations):\n- Exclude that pass from the residual data\n- For each remaining model, compute: mean, std, and the distribution of residuals\n- Check whether the summary statistics change significantly (>10% relative change in std) when any single pass is removed\n- Specifically check pass_01 (which has much larger std ~0.118 vs others ~0.02-0.03) and pass_09, pass_22 (also high variance)\n- Save per-pass-exclusion results\n\n### 3. Candidate Spectrum Cross-Pass Consistency\nThe ranked_full_parameter_grid gives a global ranking. Test whether this ranking is driven by specific passes:\n- The candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv has IC values computed over all passes combined\n- There is no per-pass candidate evaluation in the data\n- Acknowledge this limitation: you cannot do true per-pass candidate re-ranking because the data only provides combined IC\n- Instead, analyze whether the residual traces show features consistent with the rank-1 parameters (v_max=30, v_min=0.05, f_peak=1010 μHz, width=275 μHz)\n\n## Completion Conditions\n- All code must be executed (not just written)\n- Both JSON result files must contain actual numerical results\n- Your script must run without errors\n- Report any real errors encountered\n\n## Final Deliverable\nAfter saving all files, print a brief summary:\n- Number of passes analyzed\n- Whether any consistent FFT peak frequency was found\n- Whether LOO stability holds or breaks for specific passes\n- Any conflicts with the main analysis claims\n- Paths to all saved artifacts", "text": "All three JSON files are valid. The analysis is complete. Here is the summary:\n\n---\n\n**Analysis Complete -- All Files Saved to `[workspace]`**\n\n**Files saved:**\n- `fft_loo_analysis.py` -- standalone Python script (no imports of existing code)\n- `fft_results.json` -- per-pass FFT top-5 frequencies and cross-pass consistency\n- `loo_results.json` -- leave-one-pass-out robustness statistics\n- `consistency_results.json` -- candidate spectrum cross-pass consistency analysis\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. **FFT Periodicity Analysis (22 passes x 3 models):** No consistent signal near the rank-1 frequency of 1010 muHz was found in any pass. The top peak frequencies across all passes span a wide range (12,500 to 487,500 muHz). The most frequently appearing binned frequencies (37,500, 50,000, 62,500 muHz) appear at regular intervals that correspond to harmonic artifacts of the sampling grid rather than a physical periodic signal. These are likely numerical artifacts from the uniform time spacing of the data, not evidence for a real oscillation at 1010 muHz.\n\n2. **LOO Robustness -- FAILS for pass_01:** Leaving out pass_01 causes >10% relative change in residual std for all 3 models: Model A (-21%), Model B (-42%), Model C (-32%). This confirms pass_01 (std ~0.118 vs others ~0.02-0.03) is a strong outlier driving the overall variance. No other single pass produces >10% changes. LOO stability does NOT hold due to this one pass.\n\n3. **Candidate Spectrum Cross-Pass Consistency:** The rank-1 parameters (v_max=30, v_min=0.05, f_peak=1010 muHz, width=275 muHz) do not appear as an exact match in the `candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv`. The target period of 1010 muHz corresponds to ~59,400 minutes, far exceeding the time span of individual passes (~15-40 minutes), so no single pass has sufficient temporal resolution to resolve this frequency. The IC values in the spectrum comparison are computed over all passes combined, making true per-pass candidate re-ranking impossible with the available data.", "error_code": null}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:14:33.099302+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:20:20.104696+00:00"}}

id: 1142
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1142, "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "data": {"id": "c98554a8-afa8-4fa5-b873-52fb9aec0567", "run_id": "run_6be133d109564be9", "kind": "agent", "position": 483, "revision": 1142, "parent_tool_use_id": "call_3ca07f63e27345d184144b96", "agent_call_id": "46bb25c9-b57e-45ec-ae1b-e82a0bd9adf6", "data": {"status": "completed", "agent_type": "general-purpose", "sdk_agent_id": "a66e0cc031968f8e9", "task": "You are a leaf sub-Agent performing an independent verification of the spectral ranking and cross-model correlation findings for the task **Astronomy_02_gravity_spectral_structure** (T0: 2022-01-01). You must NOT delegate to any other agent, session, or model process.\n\n## Your Role and Problem\nIndependently re-compute and verify two core findings from the main analysis:\n1. **Spectral ranking integrity** — verify the delta_IC landscape, candidate counts within thresholds, and the relationship between ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv and candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv\n2. **Cross-model correlation statistics** — independently compute all pairwise correlations and verify summary statistics\n\nWrite your own code from scratch using the raw data files. Do not import or execute any existing scripts. This is a blind re-computation for verification purposes.\n\n## Project and Inputs\n- Project root: `[workspace]`\n- Data directory: `data/`\n- Read-only files:\n  - `data/pass_residual_traces.tsv` — 22 passes × ~77 time samples, 3 models (A, B, C) in mm/s\n  - `data/ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv` — 250 ranked candidates with delta_information_criterion\n  - `data/candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv` — 507 rows, two scan families (frequency_amplitude_scan, minimum_velocity_scan)\n  - `data/uniform_alternative_comparison.tsv` — 24 uniform velocity alternatives\n- Independent analysis unit: one complete time-resolved pass or one complete candidate spectral parameter combination\n\n## Required Context Reads (MUST DO BEFORE ANALYSIS)\nRead these files successfully before starting analysis:\n1. `[workspace]`\n2. `[workspace]`\n3. `[workspace]`\n4. `[workspace]`\n5. `[workspace]`\n6. `[workspace]`\n\n## Exclusive Output Directory\nSave ALL your work under: `[workspace]`\n- Create the directory if needed\n- Save your Python script as `independent_verification.py`\n- Save results as `verification_results.json`\n- Do NOT modify any files outside this directory\n\n## Analysis Requirements\n\n### 1. Spectral Ranking Verification\nFrom scratch, read `ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv` and `candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv`:\n\na) Verify the delta_information_criterion column: rank 1 has delta_IC=0 by definition. Compute what delta_IC represents (difference from rank 1? difference from some baseline?). Check whether the delta values match simple subtraction from rank 1's IC.\n\nb) Count candidates within delta_IC ≤ 10 and ≤ 20 exactly. List them.\n\nc) Investigate the relationship between the two data files:\n   - `ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv` uses column `frequency_width_microhz`\n   - `candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv` uses column `peak_width_microhz`\n   - Check if rank-1 parameters (v_max=30, v_min=0.05, f_peak=1010, width=275) exist in candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv\n   - Find the global minimum IC row in candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv and compare its parameters to rank 1\n   - Determine whether the two files represent the same candidate pool or different pools\n   - Check if there's a mapping between them\n\nd) For the uniform alternative comparison:\n   - Find the best uniform IC and its velocity\n   - Compute the IC difference (not ratio) between best structured and best uniform\n   - Assess whether the IC values from the two tables are comparable (same baseline? same likelihood function?)\n\n### 2. Cross-Model Correlation Verification\nFrom scratch, read `pass_residual_traces.tsv`:\n\na) For each of the 22 passes and each pair of models (A-B, A-C, B-C), compute Pearson r.\n\nb) Compute exact summary statistics:\n   - Mean, std, min, max for each pair\n   - Count of passes where A-B r < 0.50\n   - Count of passes where A-C r > 0.80\n   - Exact range [min, max] for B-C\n\nc) Also compute the mean absolute difference between each model pair per pass.\n\n### 3. Mode-Frequency Grid Verification\nRead `mode_frequency_grid.tsv` and verify:\n\na) For l=2, list coupling coefficients by radial order n=0..7\nb) State the actual trend direction (increasing, decreasing, or non-monotonic)\nc) Identify which radial order has the maximum coupling coefficient for l=2\nd) Check modes in the 900-1300 μHz range and count them\n\n## Completion Conditions\n- All code must be executed (not just written)\n- The JSON result file must contain actual numerical results\n- Your script must run without errors\n- Report any real errors encountered\n\n## Final Deliverable\nAfter saving all files, print a brief summary:\n- Whether your independently computed numbers match or differ from the main analysis claims\n- Any factual discrepancies found\n- Paths to all saved artifacts", "text": "Both files are saved. Here is the verification report:\n\n---\n\n## Independent Verification Report\n\n### Files Saved\n- `[workspace]` (source script)\n- `[workspace]` (full numerical results, 529 lines)\n\n### Key Findings\n\n**1. Spectral Ranking Verification**\n\n- **delta_IC definition**: Confirmed rank 1 has delta_IC=0 by definition. The delta values increase monotonically with rank. However, the delta_IC in `ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv` does NOT correspond to `IC - global_min_ic` from `candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv` -- all 29 overlapping entries show a constant offset of 43.9 (the global minimum IC), meaning `delta_IC = IC_candidate - IC_rank1_in_ranked_table`, where the ranked table uses a different baseline than the candidate comparison table.\n\n- **Threshold counts**: Exactly **4 candidates** have delta_IC <= 10 (ranks 1-4), and **9 candidates** have delta_IC <= 20 (ranks 1-9). These numbers are exact and reproducible.\n\n- **File relationship**: The two files represent **overlapping but different candidate pools**. `ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv` has 250 unique combos; `candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv` has 507 unique combos; only 29 overlap. Rank 1 params (v_max=30, v_min=0.05, f_peak=1010, width=275) do NOT exist in `candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv`. The global minimum IC in the candidate file (43.9) belongs to a different parameter set (v_max=20, v_min=0, f_peak=1010, width=300).\n\n- **Uniform alternative**: Best uniform velocity is 0.1 cm/s with IC=1522.1. The best structured IC is 43.9. The IC difference is -1478.2 (structured is much lower/better). The ranges overlap at the top end (both reach ~13553.2), suggesting they may share the same likelihood framework but different model structures.\n\n**2. Cross-Model Correlation Verification**\n\nComputed from 22 passes across all pairs:\n\n| Pair | Mean r | Std | Min | Max |\n|------|--------|-----|-----|-----|\n| A-B | 0.5730 | 0.1837 | 0.2528 | 0.9940 |\n| A-C | 0.8097 | 0.1154 | 0.5748 | 0.9973 |\n| B-C | 0.7832 | 0.1323 | 0.4892 | 0.9980 |\n\n- Passes where A-B r < 0.50: **9 out of 22**\n- Passes where A-C r > 0.80: **12 out of 22**\n- B-C range: **[0.4892, 0.9980]**\n\nMean absolute differences also computed per pair.\n\n**3. Mode-Frequency Grid Verification**\n\n- For l=2, coupling coefficients by radial order n=0..7: 2.06e-09, 5.59e-09, 6.07e-09, 8.23e-09, 8.61e-09, 7.27e-09, 4.89e-09, 2.56e-09\n- Trend: **non-monotonic** (increases from n=0 to n=4, then decreases through n=7)\n- Maximum coupling for l=2: **n=4** with coefficient 8.61e-09\n- Modes in 900-1300 microHz range: **16 modes** across angular degrees l=2 through l=8\n\n### Discrepancies Noted\n\nThe most significant finding is that `ranked_full_parameter_grid.tsv` and `candidate_spectrum_comparison.tsv` use **different candidate pools** (only 29/250 overlap), which means any direct cross-referencing between them requires careful handling. The delta_IC values cannot be directly compared to the information_criterion values in the candidate file without accounting for different baselines.", "error_code": null}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:14:50.769759+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:20:20.153507+00:00"}}

